  "body": "## What we see\n\nIn Magpie, the Grok provider card is titled `Grok (SuperGrok)`. Signed-in account badges also show `SuperGrok`.\n\nWe use two official Grok subscriptions. One is SuperGrok. The other plan name starts with H. Public docs call that tier SuperGrok Heavy ([xAI license docs](https://docs.x.ai/grok/management)). We did not capture the account API response for this report, so we do not claim which account is Heavy, or that a Heavy plan was returned and then overwritten.\n\nPlease name the provider Grok, and show the actual plan tier when it is known. Do not label every account SuperGrok.\n\n## What the current code does\n\nThese are enough to explain the card title, and the badge when `plan` is absent. They are not a capture of what the two accounts returned.\n\n- The provider display name is the fixed string `Grok (SuperGrok)`, not the signed-in plan: [`internal/provider/plugin_usage.go` line 33](https://github.com/yetone/magpie/blob/main/internal/provider/plugin_usage.go#L33).\n- The account badge uses `a.plan || \"SuperGrok\"`: [`internal/gui/assets/app.js` line 6619](https://github.com/yetone/magpie/blob/main/internal/gui/assets/app.js#L6619). If `plan` is missing, the badge is SuperGrok.\n- The community Grok plugin's `usage()` parses credit windows and does not return a plan. A comment says billing does not describe a plan's rate limit: [`packages/grok/index.mjs` around line 840](https://github.com/magpie-community/plugins/blob/main/packages/grok/index.mjs#L840). The plugin also sets the provider name to `Grok (SuperGrok)`.\n\n## Request\n\n- Show the provider as Grok, with the plan tier beside it when known.\n- When `plan` is absent, do not fall back to SuperGrok. Say the plan is unknown.\n- If the Grok plugin can read the signed-in plan, return it so the badge does not have to guess.\n\nNo account emails, quota figures, or session content are included.",
  "closed_by": null,
